n you just need a handful of  good clients you know five to ten  awesome clients will keep you busy all  year round right it'll it'll feed you  it'll feed your family and that's all  you need it's just a handful of clients  so that's the about you now the the next  thing is the real and before I talk  about the real the contact for contact  you don't have to put your phone number  it's it's up to you it's questionable if  you want to do it or not sub-q I never  did actually I did do it a couple times  but I found it wasn't necessary you just  need that email but you need it front  and center you need to let them know  easily how to contact  now let's talk about the real the real  needs to be typically 60 to 60 seconds  to 90 seconds I don't advise anything  over 90 seconds we are overloaded with  content these days and people will lose  attention the their attention to you is  going to fade very quickly after 90  seconds and and in the past I had a  small boutique motion graphic studio and  I was a creative director there and I  was hit with reels all the time and  emails with those reels that you know  attached those reels and I mean I found  that after 90 seconds I'm checked out  it's just too much you can show a lot  you can show everything about your  talent who you are in 60 90 seconds even  30 seconds works so in that reel you  need to make it unique to you and a lot  of that depends on the music and that  also shows some of your personality with  that music you need to show your best  work period if you're trying to fill up  a reel to 60 seconds and you find that  you've got 30 seconds of a really  awesome work in there and then there's  gonna be 30 seconds is kind of like  mediocre work cut it out make your reel  30 seconds okay if you if you include  the work in there that's your best work  it's gotta be also work that you want  more of because what you're doing is  you're putting your best work out there  to a producer who's looking for that  type of work and it's going that  preacher is going to hire you based on  that work so it's just a big cycle so  you are putting out to the world in your  real work that you want more of and then  that work is going to come to you I  meant for example like I don't enjoy  doing character animation okay and I  have done some character animation in  the past multiple projects and it's  because the clients I had knew what I  was capable of so they would say hey  we've got a character piece we know you  don't like doing that but can you help  us out and do that sure  I'll do it for you but I'm never going  to show that character animation on my  reel because I don't want more  that work from new clients I don't want  to advertise to the world hey I can do  character animation and then I start  getting more of that I you know I'm  filled that creatively filled with work  like particle effects and and polishes  and lighting and really dark moody work  and that's what it really fills my soul  so that's what's gonna be on my reel  because I want more of it for those of  you who are creating a reel and you  don't have enough work to really show  off your talent maybe you only have 10  15 seconds of really awesome work and  the rest of your work is stuff that you  don't want will create spec work I talk  about this all the time but my first  several reels were mostly filled with  spec work now what is spec work spec  work is not work that I'm getting from a  client I don't believe I'm not a big  believer at all and doing free work for  clients spec work for clients I've done  it a couple times in my life I'm saying  I'm literally saying two or three times  in 12 13 14 years of freelancing it  never amounted to anything it was always  the promise of hey if this turns out  awesome we're gonna going to come back  to you with all this work it's gonna be  amazing a lot of money and never works  out right I mean I same throat my  friends they've done the same thing and  it's very rare for it to come back and  be a big project it actually pays you so  I am NOT a believer in spec work you  have a unique talent as a motion  designer and you should be paid for that  even if it's a lower budget project you  should be paid something for that okay  so anyways if you don't have the work to  fill your reel make spec work go home  create the work that you want more of if  you love doing textured work or 3d work  or logo animations just create it right  like just let's say like I take a logo  like coca-cola or um you know anything  any type of make up a logo and animate  it and make it do what you think is is  just gonna sell and it's just really  awesome if people if creative directors  and producers see that and they see that  you have talent you will get hired based  on that they're not gonna come to you  and say hey did you really do this this  animation right here for  for this company they just want to see  what you've done and know that you did  it and then they're gonna hire you based  on that now that brings up one caveat  one not caveat tangent I just want to  talk about very quickly and that is  don't ever show someone else's work on  your reel that you didn't have a heavy  hand in okay this has happened to me in  the past what's happened to my friends  in the past where let's say I worked on  a 5-10 second piece and I basically did  90% of the look the design the animation  and then another four the answer came in  and he did some expressions on one  animation piece well he took that piece  and he showed it off on his reel which  is a bunch of crap right because he  didn't do that piece he did he just came  up with an expression a number to plug  into my animation so it wasn't his right  to take that piece and show it off as  his work I called him out on it told him  hey you need to remove it from your reel  and he did it right because a producer  or a creative director wants to know  that you did that piece if if you have  some really strong pieces but you're  let's say you're a specialist and all  you do is a certain piece of that image  then just just call it out you can put  that in the description below your reel  you can break down your reel and say  like you know I do particle effects  every particle effects we see in these  shots is from me let producers know be  upfront be straight with whoever it is  trying to hire you even if it's an end  consumer be straight with them they'll  appreciate it in the end and you're  going to be in a better place with them  as well okay so one more one more key  point were gonna talk about key point  number three and this is ideal clients  okay once you've got you know what could  type of freelancer you want to be you've  got some branding out there because this  your branding on the online is what is  going to attract that work to you you  need to attract your ideal client so you  need to get super clear on who your  ideal client is right you need to figure  that out so is your ideal client is it a  production studio is  it a movie studio is it an end business  is it an end consumer is it an agency  there's a lot of in clients and you need  to decide who it is that you want to  work for now maybe you don't know who it  is that you want to work for and that's  okay too it's okay to kind of dive in  and explore that but just just be aware  of who you think your ideal client  should be now for me I found that I  really enjoyed working with production  houses other teams of motion designers  so that means that production companies  like digital kitchen or prologue films  would hire me to come in and work on  these pieces for like film titles or or  advertisements and I would work for the  most part with teams of other people  maybe it's one other person maybe it's  three other guys maybe it's a whole you  know big team but I found that I really  enjoyed working with in with production  studios and producers and creative  directors I just gelled better with that  with those types of people I there was  times I worked for agencies it wasn't  for me the whole agency mentality just I  didn't gel with that and that was okay  right there's a lot of other motion  designers who do really well with that  um I also found that the times that I  worked with like an end client as like a  business you know I did a lot of that  sometimes it would work sometimes it  wouldn't work um I found a lot of times  there was maybe some higher budgets  there but it just wasn't worth it to me  I found that ultimately my sweet spot  like I said was production studios  because they they knew what my talents  were they respected my time they  respected my my creativity I got paid  really well to work with them and it was  fun I would learn a lot from other  motion designers even working remotely  like we would past project files back  and forth depending on the job and I  learned a lot that way so also with an  ideal client you need to manage  expectations okay so once you get a  client to come in to you and use your  services you need to manage what their  expectations are which is also  I found that production studios it was  much easier to do that because they did  understand that motion design takes a  lot of time let's be honest like there  are certain consumers or businesses out  there that think that the motion design  can be done really quick like you click  a button boom it's done it's animated  look at that Wow well that's not the  case right so managing expectations if  you're working with a business who  doesn't understand or even an  advertising company who doesn't  understand how long motion design takes  or they don't understand the process of  what it takes you need to manage their  expectations you need to be upfront and  say ok this is how it works this is how  I work and this is how long it's going  to take this is what's kind of cost  if managing expectations also has to do  with like things like revisions like  you're gonna get two rounds of revisions  maybe three rounds of revisions after  that the fees got to go up there's also  different stages there's a rough stage  you have to approve the rough the rough  draft of my design you have to approve  the rough draft of my animation you have  to approve the eighty-five percent  finished piece and then we're going to  polish and we're you know it's a much  easier process if you manage their  expectations and one other point about  having an ideal client is you need to  over deliver to your client and if you  respect your client you over deliver to  what their expectations are they're  going to respect you to the highest  standing and they're gonna keep coming  back for more right over delivering  doesn't mean that I'm working I'm you  know I said I would give it 40 hours and  I'm gonna give it 60 hours and call good  no that's not over delivering that's  that's killing yourself not getting paid  for it we're delivering maybe means that  the deadline is 5 o'clock on Friday I'm  going to deliver it to you at 4 o'clock  on Friday so you're you're not freaking  out the producer is not freaking out I'm  gonna deliver just right ahead of  schedule I'm going to maybe add in a few  little minut details that I know they're  going to be ok with to just really take  that piece to the next level  and and come in on budget as well coming  on time on budget and and you know added  a few little details if it's that if  it's gonna be okay to do that you don't  want to come into a piece at the very  end and say oh by the way I added this  design element right here what do you  think it's awesome although I know you  guys are gonna love it they're like what  do you mean you can't add that so it  just depends on what the client how the  client is what the project is in terms  of over-delivering but if you always  deliver on time on budget that goes  miles and miles with clients so those  are my three key points to focus on to  become a successful freelancer like I  said I get asked all the time what  should I do to become successful just  focus on the type of freelancer you want  to be that goes very deep into  software's and-and-and what it is that  you actually want to do focus on your  branding and that's your online presence  and focus on getting your ideal client  and you will be good to go so that's  what I've got for you if you want to  dive even deeper into how to become a  successful freelancer I do have a course  through motion science TV called the  freelance effect it goes into these  three key points even deeper and it goes  into three or four more points in  addition to these that go very deep it's  a course of a setup to set you up for  success as a freelancer because like I  said I've done this for many many many  years I've always charged a premium for  my services I've always gotten paid for  those services and I've got a lot of  killer work to show based on those  philosophies so I hope you enjoyed this  video and until next time I'm Cameron  and this is motion science  [Music]
 

